/-!
# The kernel of the conductor map of a boundary union

Let `Y` be a projective complex fourfold and `Q = ⋃ⱼ Qⱼ` a reduced union of distinct prime
divisors of `Y`, with normalization `ν : N_Q ⟶ Q`. Let `A_Q ⊆ Q` be a closed subset of dimension
at most two, with its reduced structure, such that `ν` is an isomorphism over `Q ∖ A_Q`, and let
`F_Q = (ν⁻¹ A_Q)_red` be its conductor preimage. Then `ν` restricts to `ι : F_Q ⟶ A_Q`, and the
kernel of `ι_* : H_4(F_Q, ℚ) ⟶ H_4(A_Q, ℚ)` has dimension `∑_V (r_V - 1)`, the sum over the
irreducible surfaces `V ⊆ Q` of the branch counts minus one.

Both `F_Q` and `A_Q` have dimension at most two, so their top homology is freely generated by
the classes of their surface components (Fulton, Lemma 19.1.1), and the class map commutes with
proper push-forward; hence `ι_*` is the push-forward of rational surface cycles (Stacks' cycles,
`MMP.Chow.RationalCycleGroup`; Tag 02R4), `[E] ↦ deg(E/ι(E)) [ι(E)]`, with every degree
`[κ(E) : κ(ι(E))]` positive. Its kernel has dimension
`∑_W (#{E : ι(E) = W} - 1)` over the surfaces `W ⊆ A_Q`. The normalization `N_Q` is the disjoint
union of the normalizations `S^ν` (Stacks, Tag 0CDV), so the surfaces of `F_Q` over `W` are the
prime divisors of the `S^ν` mapping onto `W`, and their number is `r_W`; a surface `V ⊆ Q` not
contained in `A_Q` has `r_V ≤ 1`, since `ν` is injective over `Q ∖ A_Q`.

## Main results

* `MMP.Fourfold.finrank_ker_conductorPreimage_pushforward`: `dim ker ι_* = ∑_V (r_V - 1)`.
* `MMP.Fourfold.bijective_rationalCycleClass`: on a complex scheme of dimension at most `k`, the
  class map `Z_k(X)_ℚ → H_{2k}(X, ℚ)` on Stacks' rational `k`-cycles is an isomorphism.

## Implementation notes

* `Q` is a finite set of prime divisors of `Y`, and the reduced union is any reduced closed
  subscheme `I.subscheme` of `Y` whose support is the union of their supports; a reduced closed
  subscheme is determined by its support. Its normalization is
  `MMP.Fourfold.conductorNormalization`; it is the disjoint union of the normalizations of the
  `Qⱼ`. The conductor preimage is `MMP.Fourfold.conductorPreimageScheme`.
* The map `ι : F_Q ⟶ A_Q` is any morphism with `ι ≫ (A_Q ⟶ Q) = (F_Q ⟶ N_Q) ≫ ν`; since
  `A_Q ⟶ Q` is a closed immersion, hence a monomorphism, there is exactly one, the restriction of
  `ν`. It is taken over `ℂ` and proper, which is what the proper push-forward needs.
* `A_Q` is a closed subset of the reduced union, carrying its reduced structure
  `(Scheme.IdealSheafData.vanishingIdeal A).subscheme`. The structure of `N_Q` over `ℂ` is
  `ν` followed by that of the reduced union; closed subschemes are over `ℂ` through their
  inclusions.
* `H_4(-, ℚ)` is the rational Borel–Moore homology of the complex points
  (`MMP.Fourfold.RationalHomology`); for the projective schemes `F_Q` and `A_Q` it is singular
  homology. The sum over surfaces is a `finsum`; only the surfaces of `A_Q` contribute, and there
  are finitely many of them.
* A point of height two on `S^ν` has coheight one, because `S^ν ⟶ Y` is integral and `Y` is a
  fourfold of finite type over `ℂ`; this identifies the surfaces of `F_Q` with prime divisors of
  the `S^ν`. Heights are preserved by integral morphisms (incomparability), which gives every
  dimension count used.

## References

* [W. Fulton, *Intersection Theory*][fulton1998], §19.1.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CDV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CDV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02R4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02R4)
-/

